{"parserVersion":"verification-native-parser.v1","parentDigest":"sha256:b5d7f9798de00dd73dd6f287f95876f0dce09cb085c701e630d99f14bd4f4ea1","projections":[{"kind":"pdf_text","pageCount":24,"pages":[{"physicalPageNumber":1,"text":"Advanced\nTruAge Report\nJane Doe\nAge: 29 | Sex: Female\nID#: VBC43XM\nCollected: 06/06/2025 | Reported: 06/11/2025","textLayerDigest":"sha256:2bb9a6c2a8685095da9cc32ce015e874568a106c63b9fdbb63da3beaaf0ccbaf","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":2,"text":"Aging Summary\nChronological Age: 29.4\nOMICm Age\n14.1\nYounger\nSymphony Age\n22.6\nYounger\nPace of Aging\n0.7\nSlower\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 01","textLayerDigest":"sha256:c93fc6ee45a98cd8037952e8537ba59ecc41bd0e05e9e6b39e6004cf0c8c7289","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":3,"text":"DEVELOPED WITH HARVARD\nOMICm Age Chronological Age: 29.4\nOMICm Age\n14.1\nYounger\n10.2%\nYour OMICm Age is lower than 89.8% of\npeople of the same age and sex.\nResults Over Time\nDISCLAIMER: The population graph and percentile for OMICm Age are based on observed and validated data patterns from an equal distribution of Harvard research participants\nand TruDiagnostic clients to emulate a population of average health.\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 02","textLayerDigest":"sha256:8d1975704e0287029877eae1676a1901761d258e15a251be8e4af6caedee17d6","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":4,"text":"Most Actionable Epigenetic Biomarkers\nEpigenetic Biomarker (EBs) are compared against a balanced reference dataset composed of individuals from both the Harvard and TruDiagnostic\ncohorts. We identify individuals of the same sex and a similar age (within ±5 years) to create a personalized comparison group. Above the 80th\npercentile: Indicates your outcome is higher than 80% of individuals in the cohort. Below the 20th percentile: Indicates your outcome is lower than\n80% of individuals in the cohort.\n* Listed in order of impact on OMICm age. Find the rest of your Epigenetic Biomarkers at the end of the report\nCystine High 87.9%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nCystine: A disulfide form of cysteine; indicates oxidative stress Avoid excessive methionine-rich protein sources like eggs, dairy, certain nuts, and\nand protein turnover. seeds\nStay hydrated\nIncrease vitamins B6, B9, and B12 to support homocysteine metabolism\nVitamin B6 Vitamin B9 Vitamin B12\nMimecan High 87.6%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nMimecan (Osteoglycin): A small leucine-rich proteoglycan; Consider an anti-inflammatory diet\ninvolved in bone mineralization and cardiovascular health. Manage stress\nMaintain an active lifestyle\nIf applicable, manage underlying cardiovascular or fibrotic conditions\nMonitor blood pressure and cholesterol\nOmega-3 Fatty Acid\n1-Stearoyl-2-adrenoyl-GPC (18:0/22:4)* Low 14.3%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nA glycerophosphocholine (GPC) lipid, this compound supports If applicable, correct omega-3 deficiency\nmembrane integrity, brain function, and lipid metabolism, Increase antioxidants\nessential for longevity. Low levels may impair neuroprotection, Limit alcohol\nincrease oxidative stress, and accelerate age-related decline.\nOmega-3 Fatty Acid Flaxseeds Walnuts Fatty fish\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 03","textLayerDigest":"sha256:3e2f2902005268d8e2d6b4ac16c51df22e0bf96a0a21b8c5dc32fedf1864b9fa","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":5,"text":"DEVELOPED WITH YALE\nSYMPHONY Age Chronological Age: 29.4\nThis advanced approach dives into the age of 11 distinct\norgan systems.\nSYMPHONY Age\n22.6\nYounger\n6.8%\nYour SYMPHONY Age is lower than 93.2%\nof people of the same age and sex.\nResults Over Time\nDISCLAIMER: The population graph and percentile for SYMPHONY Age are based on observed and validated data patterns from an equal distribution of Harvard research\nparticipants and TruDiagnostic clients to emulate a population of average health.\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 04","textLayerDigest":"sha256:b333f43936eabf59711b28f253e5bb3c3d3708d35dfc1e4d4edf9125c89c752d","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":6,"text":"Compare the age of 11 distinct organ systems\nversus your chronological age\nBelow Chronological Age Chronological Age Above Chronological Age\n29.4\nSYMPHONY Age 22.6\nBlood 21.7\nBrain 23.7\nInflammation 18.9\nHeart 21.3\nHormone 29.3\nImmune 24.2\nKidney 19.3\nLiver 26.4\nMetabolic 22.9\nLung 27.3\nMusculoskeletal 18.0\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 05","textLayerDigest":"sha256:fabbcb3e97b3390ef17ca23827a8b771a3265ff2c337ceeb5e36b1efa8ddc631","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":7,"text":"How was this clock created and designed?\nMany clocks have used blood based biomarkers and their changes as we get older to predict OMICm age. For SYMPHONY Age, the researchers did\nthis with biomarkers which were specific for each organ system. While previous clocks (like PhenoAge) did this with 9 blood markers, SYMPHONY\nAge incorporates 133 biomarkers for training to develop this clock. In addition to having a large and detailed number of clinical datapoints, this study\nis also one of the largest for clock development with approximately 8,000 participants. Together, this gives us an incredibly robust clock associated\nwith organ specific clinical biomarkers which is highly predictive of aging outcomes.\nSystems Related Biomarkers\nBlood\nBiomarkers: Ferritin, Hematocrit, Hemoglobin, Mean Corpuscular Hemoglobin,\nMean Corpuscular Hemoglobin Conc, Mean Corpuscular Volume, Mean Platelet\nVolume, Platelet Distribution width, Platelet Count, Red Blood Cell Count, Red Cell\nDistribution Width\nBrain\nBiomarkers: Homocysteine, BDNF (serum), Clusterin, Stroke, Total mental status\nsummary score, Total cognition summary score, Immediate word recall, Delayed\nword recall, Total word recall summary score, Serial 7s\nInflammation\nBiomarkers: Ferritin, C-Reactive Protein, Transforming Growth Factor Beta,\nInterleukin 10, Interleukin 1 Receptor Antagonist, Interleukin 6, Tumor Necrosis\nFactor Receptor 1\nHeart\nBiomarkers: Shortness of breath while awake, PCcomponents of Grimage,\nPrevious High Blood Pressure, Previous Heart Attack, Previous Stroke,\nHomocysteine, BMI\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 06","textLayerDigest":"sha256:2a78a9ca3c1d62cd430228ceda6969152b7df8bc7fdd960fddbd93a7d351727f","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":8,"text":"Hormone\nBiomarkers: IGF-1, DHEAS\nImmune\nBiomarkers: Eosinophil Count, Lymphocyte Count, Monocyte Count, Neutrophil\nCount, Percent Basophils, Percent Eosinophils, Percent Lymphocytes, Percent\nMonocytes, White Blood Cell Count, Myeloid Dendritic cells (DC-M) Percentage,\nPlasmacytoid, Dendritic Cells (DC-P) Percentage, NK Cells: CD56HI Percentage,\nNK Cells: CD56LO Percentage, CD16- Monocytes Percentage, CD16+ Monocytes\nPercentage, B Cells Percentage, CD8+ T Cells: Central Memory (CM) Percentage,\nCD4+ T Cells: Central Memory (CM) Percentage, CD8+ T Cells Percentage, CD8+ T\nCells: (TemRA) Percentage, CD4+ T Cells: (TemRA) Percentage, CD4+ T Cells\nPercentage, IgD+ Memory B Cells Percentage, IgD- Memory B Cells Percentage,\nCD8+ T Cells: Naïve Percentage, CD4+ T Cells: Naïve Percentage, T Cells\nPercentage, Naive B Cells Percentage, CD8+T Cells: Effector Memory (Tem)\nPercentage, CD4+ T Cells: Effector Memory (Tem) Percentage, Natural Killer (NK)\nCells Percentage, Monocytes Percentage, Dendritic Cells Percentage\nKidney\nBiomarkers: Albumin, Urea Nitrogen, Chloride, Bicarbonate, Creatinine, Cystatin C,\nPotassium, Sodium\nLiver\nBiomarkers: Albumin, Alkaline Phosphatase, ALT, AST, Bilirubin, Total Protein\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 07","textLayerDigest":"sha256:14405c045aac09fa46ed89e6ffc203866821c1018fb913242483d0847af8c89c","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":9,"text":"Metabolic\nBiomarkers: PCSmoking-packyears, Previous Diabetes, C-Reactive Protein,\nGlucose-Fasting, HDL-Cholesterol, LDL-Cholesterol, Triglycerides, Interleukin-6,\nBMI, Waist circumference\nLung\nBiomarkers: Peak expiratory flow, bicarbonate, chronic lung disease, shortness of\nbreath while awake, persistent wheezing, cough, or bringing up phlegm,\nPCSmoking-packyears\nMusculoSkeletal\nBiomarkers: Vitamin D3, Dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate, IGF-1, Arthritis,\nHeight, Weight, BMI, some diff-mobility, hand grip strength maximum\nmeasurement, semi tandem balance test time, timed walk test time, hand grip\nstrength-left hand hand grip strength-right hand, had back problems, some diff-\nstoop/kneel/crouch, diff-stoop/kneel/crouch, diff-walk one block, diff-walk sev\nblocks, some diff-walk one block, some diff-walk sev blocks, diff-climb sev flt\nstair, diff-climb one flt stair, some diff-clmb sev flt str, some diff-clmb 1 flt stair,\ndiff-get up fr chair, some diff-get up fr chair, diff-reach/extnd arms up, some diff-\nrch/xtnd arms up, diff-lift/carry 10lbs, some diff-lift/carry 10lbs, side-by-side\nbalance test time, full tandem balance test time, Sum of 7 different functional\ntests, Combination of all balance scores\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 08","textLayerDigest":"sha256:a940733e3a7145951d1850340bb2f80d7ac7d67ecd898ca65aefdd900a0ea966","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":10,"text":"DEVELOPED WITH DUKE\nDunedinPACE of Aging Chronological Age: 29.4\nYour Rate of Aging will change based on your lifestyle interventions.\nBe sure to retest every 3 months to track your progress.\n0.7\n0.45 1.6\nYour score < 1\nEach year that passes, your body will\nbiologically age 0.7 years .\n*Based on the last 2-3 months\nResults Over Time\nDISCLAIMER: The population graph for DunedinPACE of Aging is based on observed and validated data patterns from an equal distribution of Harvard research participants and\nTruDiagnostic clients to emulate a population of average health.\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 09","textLayerDigest":"sha256:2195b9acaef2fd5b6da4be61317cdf6ad20d6d4dbd098782cf014ad4eb45bebc","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":11,"text":"Immune Health\nNaïve CD4T Normal 68.6%\nMemory CD4T Normal 69.2%\nMemory CD8T Normal 44.1%\nNaïve CD8T Normal 83.1%\nBasophils Normal 83.1%\nB Memory Normal 61.9%\nNaïve B Normal 68.6%\nRegulatory T Normal 78.0%\nEosinophils Normal 41.5%\nNatural Killer Normal 88.0%\nNeutrophils Normal 25.4%\nMonocyte Normal 83.9%\nCD4T / CD8T Ratio Normal 49.1%\nCD4/CD8T cell ratio is incredibly informative on disease. A low or inverted CD4/CD8 ratio is an immune risk phenotype and is associated with altered immune function, immune\nsenescence, and chronic inflammation.\nNeutrophil To Lymphocyte Normal 22.9%\nThe Neutrophil-to-Lymphocyte Ratio (NLR) is obtained by dividing the number of neutrophils by the number of lymphocytes. During physiological stress, neutrophil count increases while\nlymphocyte count decreases. Physiological stress, driven by illness, inflammation, or psychological stress, can elevate NLR. Therefore, NLR elevation is not exclusive to infection or\ninflammation but can result from any form of physiological stress, including everyday stress and poor recovery or stress management.\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 10","textLayerDigest":"sha256:e2f6f061be7ab7f22f1a45db97cfed43ce5a77c2fd484750539e5634b6b0f372","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":12,"text":"Telomere Length\nChronological Age: 29.4\nShorter telomere length and low telomerase activity are correlated\nwith several chronic preventable diseases.\nTelomere Biological Age\n20.2\nYounger\nTelomere Length\n7.6\n5.5 8.5\nResults Over Time\nDISCLAIMER: Telomere length is mostly determined based on predetermined genetic function. The population graph is based on observed and validated data patterns from an equal\ndistribution of Harvard research participants and TruDiagnostic clients to emulate a population of average health.\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 11","textLayerDigest":"sha256:d8ff92e5aa0061420e78aafe81a4361e88913021eebb0b7c799ff8a33f29975a","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":13,"text":"Inflammation\nCRP\n15.4%\nYour CRP methylation level is lower than 84.6% of the\npopulation at your same age and sex.\nCRP is produced by the liver in response to acute inflammation.\nDNAm CRP has an inverse relationship with cognitive functions\nsuch as memory, speed, and visuospatial functions.\nIL-6\n21.2%\nYour IL-6 methylation level is lower than 78.8% of the\npopulation at your same age and sex.\nIL-6 is a widely used marker of inflammation and circulating levels\nof the cytokine typically rise in older age. DNAm IL-6 is positively\nassociated with BMI, self-reported smoking status, and alcohol\nintake.\nDISCLAIMER: The population graph and percentile are based on observed and validated data patterns from an equal distribution of Harvard research participants and TruDiagnostic\nclients to emulate a population of average health.\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 12","textLayerDigest":"sha256:e0f5e683664ce8fed39f07701df759494779e56d15b615da99ea6b192abfa246","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":14,"text":"DEVELOPED WITH HARVARD\nOMICm Fit Age Chronological Age: 29.4\nTells you how old you are according to your physical fitness and\nfunctionality.\nFor every one year older OMICm FitAge is, there is an average 0.29 decrease in relative grip strength and 0.32 increase in BMI. OMICm FitAge has estimated that\nhigh-fit individuals (classified through VO2max) have a 1.5 to 2.0 younger OMICm age compared to low/medium fit individuals in females and males, respectively.\nYounger OMICm FitAge was associated with better memory test performance, emphasizing the beneficial role of physical exercise on cognitive health.\nFit Age\n23.6\nYounger\n-5.8 yr\nYour FitAge is lower by 5.8 years compared to\npeople your same age and sex.\nGrip Strength Percentile\n78.0%\nYour Grip Strength is higher than 78.0% of people\nof the same age and sex.\nDISCLAIMER: The population graph and percentile are based on observed and validated data patterns from an equal distribution of Harvard research participants and TruDiagnostic\nclients to emulate a population of average health. These are our least correlated clocks but can be used for most to gamify fitness.\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 13","textLayerDigest":"sha256:b3e66916b0dedde61a92e0829018316677b69b1d4017578eb5058398489899d9","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":15,"text":"Gait Speed Percentile\n87.3%\nYour Gait Speed is higher than 87.3% of people of\nthe same age and sex.\nGait speed, also known as walking speed, is measured in meters\nper second.\nVO2 Max Percentile\n55.6%\nYour VO2 Max is higher than 55.6% of people of\nthe same age and sex.\nMaximal oxygen uptake, or VO2max, is a measure of\ncardiovascular health and aerobic endurance.\nFEV1 Percentile\n86.3%\nYour FEV1 is higher than 86.3% of people of the\nsame age and sex.\nForced expiratory volume, also known as FEV1, measures lung\nfunction by determining the amount of air forced from the lungs\nin one second.\nDISCLAIMER: The population graph and percentile are based on observed and validated data patterns from an equal distribution of Harvard research participants and TruDiagnostic\nclients to emulate a population of average health.\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 14","textLayerDigest":"sha256:b3ba037a3faacb8f351854fb1f128d5aa6dc7170c867d535bb614fd8ce903059","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":16,"text":"RelativeSmoking Risk\nHigh Risk 80+\nTobacco Smoking Epigenetic Predictor\nLow Risk 1.6%\nIntermediate Risk 70 - 80\n70 80 Low Risk 0 - 70\nRelativeAlcohol Risk\nHigh Risk 80+\nAlcohol Epigenetic Biomarker\nHigh Risk 83.1%\nIntermediate Risk 70 - 80\n70 80 Low Risk 0 - 70\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 15","textLayerDigest":"sha256:4894df1f1d9b92f720600b3d94b24f20a3fa2001525bb61d24291d5b832f1eef","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":17,"text":"Additional Epigenetic Biomarkers\nAlbumin Normal 95%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nAlbumin: A protein in the blood that helps maintain oncotic If applicable, consume high-proten foods\npressure and transport substances. Diversify protein sources\nConsider vitamin D and omega-3-rich foods or supplements.\nManage liver and kidney health\nLimit nephrotoxic medications\nVitamin D Omega-3 Fatty Acids Lean meats Poultry Fish\nEggs Dairy Legumes Nuts\nHemoglobin Normal 94.8%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nHemoglobin: A protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen; Correct nutrient deficiencies, especially folate, iron, B12, and vitamin C.\ncritical for evaluating oxygen transport and anemia. Consider EPO in the setting of CKD\nManage chronic conditions\nStay active and hydrated\nVitamin B12 Folate Iron Vitamin C\nLeafy green vegetables Citrus fruits Legumes Eggs\nHbA1c Normal 6.8%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nHbA1c: Glycated hemoglobin; indicates average blood sugar Consume low-glycemic balanced meals\nlevels over the past 2–3 months. Exercise regularly\nPursue weight loss if applicable\nReduce sugar intake\nManage stress\nConsider medications if advised by your physician\nConsider supplements like ALA, berberine, or dihydroberberine\nAlpha Lipoic Acid Berberine Dihydroberberine Lean proteins\nHealthy fats Fiber-rich foods\nHematocrit Normal 85.9%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nHematocrit: Percentage of red blood cells in blood; used to Correct nutrient deficiencies, especially folate, iron, B12, and vitamin C\nassess anemia and hydration. Treat illness\nStay active and hydrated\nVitamin B12 Folate Iron Vitamin C\nLeafy green vegetables Citrus fruits Legumes Eggs\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 16","textLayerDigest":"sha256:7dec44acf1b9132e6bcad44c11ddd0ce15538442927b900163d3516a13bb51d9","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":18,"text":"Additional Epigenetic Biomarkers\nBlood Urea Nitrogen Normal 52.0%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nBlood Urea Nitrogen (BUN): Measures urea levels in the blood, Hydrate regularly\nindicating kidney function and protein metabolism. If applicable, moderate protein intake\nManage any kidney or liver disease\nAvoid nephrotoxic drugs\nAlkaline Phosphatase Normal 6.7%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nAlkaline Phosphatase: An enzyme found in the liver, bones, and Correct Vitamin D deficiency\nother tissues; elevated levels indicate liver or bone issues. Treat liver or bone disease if applicable\nEat a diet low in saturated fats\nExercise regularly\nAvoid alcohol and toxins\nVitamin D\nInsulin-like Growth Factor-Binding Protein 2 Normal 60.8%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nInsulin-Like Growth Factor-Binding Protein 2 (IGFBP2): Regulates Regularly practice calorie restriction under healthcare provider guidance\ninsulin-like growth factor activity; linked to metabolism and Engage in aerobic and resistance exercise to improve insulin sensitivity\naging. Consume balanced meals\nManage stress\nReduce excessive protein intake\nImprove sleep to support growth hormone function\nCarboxypeptidase B2 Normal 22.7%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nCarboxypeptidase B2: An enzyme involved in clot dissolution Consume adequate protein\nand complement regulation. Correct any nutrient deficiencies, especially vitamin K and omega-3\nControl inflammation through an anti-inflammatory diet and regular exercise\nOmega-3 Fatty Acid Vitamin K\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 17","textLayerDigest":"sha256:d7efb6ba84afb6ed6464b945d3328d6b94387d2efc56b4732862d9b24cc174ab","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":19,"text":"Additional Epigenetic Biomarkers\nSerum Paraoxonase/arylesterase 1 Normal 94.8%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nSerum Paraoxonase/Arylesterase 1 (PON1): An enzyme with Increase selenium intake with foods like Brazil nuts, seafood, and whole foods\nantioxidant properties; linked to cardiovascular health. Consider chokeberry extract and pomegranate\nMinimize intake of trans fat\nChokeberry extract Brazil nuts Seafood Pomegranate\nRibonuclease pancreatic Normal 62.9%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nRibonuclease Pancreatic: An enzyme that degrades RNA; linked Consider an anti-inflammatory diet\nto digestive and cellular processes. Regulate blood sugar levels\nManage stress\nMaintain an active lifestyle\nOmega-3 Fatty Acid Anti-inflammatory diet\nInter-alpha-trypsin Inhibitor Heavy Chain H3 Normal 0%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nInter-Alpha-Trypsin Inhibitor Heavy Chain H3: A protein involved Identify and control sources of inflammation, especially insulin resistance, gut\nin inflammation and tissue repair. microbiome imbalances, dietary factors, lipids, toxins, autoimmunity, and\nenvironmental exposures\nMaintain an active lifestyle\nConsider an anti-inflammatory diet\nAnti-inflammatory diet\nFasting Glucose Normal 9.8%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nGlucose: Blood sugar level; essential for assessing metabolic Control carbohydrated intake when applicable\nhealth and diagnosing diabetes. Stay active\nManage weight\nAvoid sugar\nManage stress\nConsider medications if advised by your physician\nConsider supplements like ALA, berberine, or dihydroberberine\nAlpha Lipoic Acid Berberine Dihydroberberine Lean proteins\nHealthy fats Fiber-rich foods\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 18","textLayerDigest":"sha256:0ecfcdd253a07bfba8e3db5edd1e0f08645b80d3503b224b667b9dcc65bdd03c","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":20,"text":"Additional Epigenetic Biomarkers\nRibitol Normal 5.4%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nRibitol: A sugar alcohol; involved in pentose phosphate and Limit sugar and high glycemic food intake\nmetabolic pathways. Consider monitoring blood sugar\nLean protein Low fat dairy\nVanillactic Acid Normal 7.1%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nVanillactate: A product of gut microbial metabolism of phenolic Manage blood pressure\ncompounds; involved in energy pathways. Reduce sodium intake\nEat a heart-healthy diet\nReduce stress\nCheck heart function\nCreatinine Normal 63.3%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nCreatinine: A byproduct of muscle metabolism; used to evaluate Hydrate regularly\nkidney function. If applicable, reduce red meat intake\nControl diabetes and hypertension\nAvoid nephrotoxic drugs\nNote that creatine can affect this level\nPhenylacetylglutamine Normal 17.6%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nPhenylacetylglutamine: A gut microbiome-derived metabolite of Balance protein intake\nphenylalanine; linked to kidney function. Limit foods high in phenylalanine (e.g, processed foods, dairy, meat, and some\ngrains)\nStay hydrated\nMonitor liver and kidney function\nConsider prebiotics or probiotics for gut health\nPrebiotics Probiotics\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 19","textLayerDigest":"sha256:6e75cae9b3d078e6ca38f07281fd454491c74f67f8c1c6ef94e31f69a2bba588","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":21,"text":"Additional Epigenetic Biomarkers\nUridine Normal 59.3%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nUridine: A nucleoside involved in RNA synthesis and energy Incorporate uridine-rich foods like tomatoes, broccoli, and mushrooms, which are\nmetabolism. synergistic with choline and DHA\nUridine Choline DHA Tomatoes Broccoli\nMushrooms Eggs Fatty fish\n1-margaroyl-glycerophospholipid(GPE)(17:0)* Normal 38.8%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\n1-Margaroyl-Glycerophospholipid (GPE) (17:0): A If applicable, reduce saturated fat intake\nglycerophospholipid; essential for cell membrane stability. Increase omega-3 intake\nConsider anti-inflammatory agents like curcumin or green tea extract\nOmega-3 Fatty Acid Curcumin Green tea extract Green tea\n4-Methoxyphenol Sulfate Normal 52%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\n4-Methoxyphenol Sulfate: A sulfated metabolite of phenolic Avoid woodsmoke exposure and dark-aged beer\ncompounds; reflects detoxification processes. Avoid synthetic fragrances\nN-acetyl-isoputreanine Normal 51.6%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nN-Acetyl-Isoputreanine: A metabolite of polyamine biosynthesis; Consider NAC supplementation\nassociated with cellular growth and repair.\nNAC\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 20","textLayerDigest":"sha256:8ac8dd0207d9572d82df4af40459dc1e3f2dfe5c977febf9f9ad383507ac7620","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":22,"text":"Additional Epigenetic Biomarkers\nGluconate Normal 22.2%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nGluconate: A metabolite of glucose; involved in carbohydrate Cut added sugar, added fat, and processed foods\nmetabolism and energy production. Exercise regularly\nConsider ALA, berberine, or dihydroberberine\nLimit wine\nAlpha Lipoic Acid Berberine Dihydroberberine\nCarotene Diol Normal 78.0%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nCarotene Diol: A carotenoid metabolite; an antioxidant linked to Consume more carotenoid-rich foods like carrots, kale, and spinach\nvitamin A activity. Important for eye and brain health.\nCarrots Kale Spinach\n4-hydroxyphenylacetylglutamine Normal 33.5%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\n4-Hydroxyphenylacetylglutamine: A gut microbial metabolite of Manage blood pressure\ntyrosine; linked to gut health and kidney function. If applicable, reduce protein intake\nIncrease fiber and fermented foods\nConsider prebiotics or probiotics\nStay hydrated\nProtect kidney health\nPrebiotics Probiotics Kefir Yogurt Sauerkraut\nFiber-rich foods\nAndrosterone Sulfate Normal 93.5%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nAndrosterone Sulfate: A steroid hormone metabolite Correct any nutrient deficiencies, especially zinc, magnesium, vitamin D, and\ndownstream of testosterone; reflects androgen activity and omega-3\nmetabolism. Optimize sleep\nManage stress\nConsider hormone therapy if needed\nMaintain an active lifestyle\nZinc Magnesium Vitamin D Omega-3 DHEA\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 21","textLayerDigest":"sha256:b7ffc692368151469dcf2beab073c62bd571beee37891fa38e8a4829811a017c","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":23,"text":"Additional Epigenetic Biomarkers\n3-Ureidopropionate Normal 1%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\n3-Ureidopropionate: A product of pyrimidine metabolism; If applicable, reduce excessive protein intake, especially those rich in pyrimidines\ninvolved in nitrogen recycling. like organ meats\nStay hydrated\nMonitor kidney and liver health\nMatrix-remodeling-associated protein 5 Normal 46.4%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nMatrix-Remodeling-Associated Protein 5: A protein involved in Reduce general systemic inflammation through an anti-inflammatory diet and an\nextracellular matrix remodeling and tissue repair. active lifestyle\nReduce stress\nAnti-inflammatory diet\nBone Morphogenetic protein 1 Normal 77.1%\n20 100\nFunction Recommendations\nBone Morphogenetic Protein 1 (BMP1): A protein involved in Consume calcium-rich and vitamin D-rich foods\nbone formation and extracellular matrix remodeling. Consider calcium or vitamin D supplementation\nIncrease weight-bearing exercise\nEnsure adequate protein intake\nCalcium Vitamin D Green leafy vegetables Nuts Dairy\nFatty fish\nVersican core protein Normal 26.8%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nVersican Core Protein: A proteoglycan in the extracellular matrix; Avoid being underweight\ninvolved in tissue remodeling and inflammation. Increase moderate-intensity exercise\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 22","textLayerDigest":"sha256:bbbb4f22849809d1c34571c5a462f339625fe85d1894bfde93a506b5185bae0c","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12},{"physicalPageNumber":24,"text":"Additional Epigenetic Biomarkers\nHistone H2B type 1-K Normal 29.9%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nHistone H2B Type 1-K: A histone protein involved in chromatin Limit UV exposure\nstructure and epigenetic regulation. Increase antioxidant intake\nAvoid smoke exposure\nManage stress\nMaintain an active lifestyle\nRed Blood Cell Distribution Width Normal 15.3%\n0 80\nFunction Recommendations\nRed Blood Cell Width (RDW): Measures the variation in red blood Correct nutrient deficiencies, especially B12, folate, iron, and vitamin C\ncell size. Manage chronic illness and inflammation\nConsider omega-3s\nAvoid alcohol and smoking\nVitamin B12 Folate Iron Vitamin C Omega-3 Fatty Acids\nRed meat Beans Fortified cereals Dairy Meat\nLeafy green vegetables Citrus fruits Tomatoes Peppers\nJane Doe TruDiagnostic.com 23","textLayerDigest":"sha256:e0914dd19da53b81f6d559e53012332ef6ae6a906805ada6dabdc59777d831ac","widthPoints":841.91998,"heightPoints":1191.12}],"residuals":[{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":1,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":2,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":3,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":4,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":5,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":6,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":7,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":8,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":9,"detail":"Visual evidence requires a separately admitted visual route."},{"kind":"unresolved_visual_content","physicalPageNumber":10,"detail":"Visual evidenc